<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Société Générale de Belgique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1822–2003</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Société Générale de Belgique was a bank and holding company founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1822 by King William I of the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>. After Belgian independence it financed railways, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines and steelworks, becoming one of the first great investment banks of continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It later held vast interests in the Belgian Congo and dominated much of the Belgian economy until it was taken over by the French group Suez in 1988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/societe-generale-de-belgique.jpg" alt="Société Générale de Belgique" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boubloub · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brussels_Royale_30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
